##### send 'h' at +40.6 s
Homing toward the POSITIVE end, switch active high. Approach 0.200 rev/s, then creep off at 0.010 rev/s...
  approach:
    outcome: STOPPED BY THE SWITCH
    from:    263 usteps (1.849 deg, 0.0051 rev)
    to:      3568 usteps (25.087 deg, 0.0697 rev)
    commanded 3305 usteps, encoder measured 3098 usteps
    follow ratio: 0.9374
    elapsed: 526 ms
  stage 1 XLATCH, closing edge: 3568 usteps (25.087 deg, 0.0697 rev)
  stage 1 settled:              3568 usteps (25.087 deg, 0.0697 rev)
    overshoot past the closing latch: 0 usteps
      A hard stop latches and stops at the same position,
      so zero here is correct. It does NOT include the
      debounce delay, which elapses before the driver sees
      anything -- that is what stage 2 is for.
  stage 2 XLATCH, release edge: 2824 usteps (19.856 deg, 0.0552 rev)
    creep travelled -768 usteps at 0.010 rev/s
    closing to release: 744 usteps (5.231 deg)
      That is the approach's debounce overshoot plus the
      switch's own throw. It grows with approach velocity,
      which is why the release edge is the datum and the
      closing edge is not.
  backed off to -1304 usteps; switch released
  [ ok ] homed. Position 0 is the RELEASE edge, taken at
  the creep velocity, and the encoder was zeroed there too.
  total: 2626 ms


##### send 'H' at +47.3 s
Homing repeatability, 5 runs. Each run re-homes from the previous back-off.
One throwaway home runs first and is NOT counted: the first
home of a series starts from wherever the last command left
the mechanism, so including it measures approach history
rather than repeatability. Measured 2026-09-02: with the
first run counted, one series read 5 counts and another 2,
while the settled runs within each agreed to about 1.
Every run re-zeroes at its own switch point, so the
run-to-run scatter shows up as spread in the encoder reading
at the moment of the latch.

  throwaway home done; starting the counted series.

run 1:
  release ENC_LATCH 38 usteps, closing-to-release 719 usteps, 2503 ms
run 2:
  release ENC_LATCH 38 usteps, closing-to-release 719 usteps, 2505 ms
run 3:
  release ENC_LATCH 25 usteps, closing-to-release 717 usteps, 2504 ms
run 4:
  release ENC_LATCH 38 usteps, closing-to-release 714 usteps, 2504 ms
run 5:
  release ENC_LATCH 38 usteps, closing-to-release 718 usteps, 2504 ms

succeeded: 5 of 5
spread: 13 usteps (0.091 deg, 1.0 encoder counts)
  Record this in ../mouse-joystick-pcb/docs/bringup-log.md
  under Homing, WITH the homing velocity: the MAX6816's
  debounce delay scales the offset with speed, so a
  repeatability figure without a velocity means nothing.


